<%# One work in a list. Rendered by the archive (/days) and by a reader's
    collection (/collection), which are the same object seen twice: a work, a
    date, a recognition mark.

    Locals:
      painting:  the work
      pick:      its day, or nil when the work has no published Daily Pick —
                 either it was never one (kept from `/feed`, story 0020) or
                 the curator removed the day since. The two are
                 indistinguishable in the data and this row does not try to
                 tell them apart.
      current:   the pick the front door is showing, so a link never points at a
                 URL that immediately 301s (see ApplicationHelper#day_link_path)
      eager:     load the thumbnail eagerly (the first few rows only)

    Every row links, and always has since story 0030 gave every painting its
    own permanent address: `day_path` when a pick exists (richer — hand-
    written blurb, dated context), `painting_path` when it does not. `/days`
    iterates published picks, so it never reaches the fallback; `/collection`
    is the only caller that does. Unkeeping a fallback row happens by opening
    it, the same way every other row already worked — there is no second,
    row-level way to let a work go any more.

    The whole row is one link, and its accessible name carries date (when
    there is one), title and artist: a list of bare titles is useless to
    somebody navigating by link. `size: 240` and not the original — ISSUE-001
    (commit 2da2f7a) shipped full-size JPEGs into 112px boxes once already. %>
<li class="days__day">
  <a class="days__link"
     href="<%= pick ? day_link_path(pick, current) : painting_path(painting) %>"
     aria-label="<%= "#{"#{pick.scheduled_on.to_fs(:daily_long)}: " if pick}#{painting.title}, #{painting.artist_display}" %>">
    <%= render "days/row_body", painting: painting, pick: pick, current: current, eager: eager %>
  </a>
</li>
